/** @page xmlserialization XML Serialization and Deserialization

    @tableofcontents

    This page describes automatic conversion between %Qore data and XML strings with
    @ref Qore::Xml::make_xml() "make_xml()" and @ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml() "parse_xml()"; for iterative or
    user-controlled XML parsing, see @ref xmlclasses "the XML classes".

    XML serialization (conversion from %Qore data structures to XML strings) in the xml module relies on the fact
    that %Qore hashes retain insertion order, which means that conversion to and from %Qore data structures and XML
    strings can be done without data loss and without reordering the XML elements. In general, XML serialization is
    relatively straighforward, but there are a few issues to be aware of, particularly regarding element attributes
    and lists. These issues are described in the following paragraphs.

    First, a straightforward example:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> h = {
    "record": {
        "name": {
            "first": "Fred", "last": "Smith",
        },
    },
};
printf("%s\n", make_xml(h, XGF_ADD_FORMATTING));@endcode

    This produces the following result

    @verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<record>
  <name>
    <first>Fred</first>
    <last>Smith</last>
  </name>
</record>@endverbatim

    @section xmlwhitespaceserialization Preserving Character Whitespace

    Use @ref Qore::Xml::XPF_PRESERVE_WHITESPACE together with @ref Qore::Xml::XPF_PRESERVE_ORDER to retain whitespace
    between children even when no other text is present. This is needed when forwarding generic or
    schema-defined mixed XML. It also retains whitespace under xml:space="default". The WSDL module
    uses this mode and lets schema-aware conversion distinguish mixed from element-only content.

    Scalar text retains spaces, tabs and line breaks, including values containing only whitespace.
    Mixed text and CDATA retain their whitespace in the ordered \c ^value^ / \c ^cdata^ representation.
    Without XPF_PRESERVE_WHITESPACE, when an element contains child elements and no ordinary text or CDATA, the data
    projection omits
    indentation. The inherited XML attribute \c xml:space="preserve" retains that whitespace;
    \c xml:space="default" restores the usual data projection in a subtree.
    Use \c XPF_PRESERVE_ORDER when text and repeated children must retain their relative positions.

    XML parsers normalize literal carriage returns according to XML 1.0 section 2.11. Generation
    therefore writes text carriage returns as character references, preserving the caller's value:
    @code{.py}
string source = "first\r\nsecond";
string xml = make_xml({"message": source});
@assert(parse_xml(xml).message == source);
    @endcode

    @section xmlattributeserialization Serializing and Deserializing XML Attributes

    To set XML attributes, the %Qore value must be a hash and the attributes are stored in another hash in the key
    \c "^attributes^". That is; the value of the \c "^attributes^" key must be a hash, and each member of this hash
    will represent an attribute-value pair.

    For example:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> h = {
    "record": {
        "^attributes^": {"type": "customer"},
        "name": {
            "first": "Fred", "last": "Smith",
        },
    },
};
printf("%s\n", make_xml(h, XGF_ADD_FORMATTING));@endcode

    This produces the following results:

    @verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<record type="customer">
  <name>
    <first>Fred</first>
    <last>Smith</last>
  </name>
</record>
    @endverbatim

    If instead we wanted to have text instead of child data under the "record" node, we must set the \c "^value^" key
    of the hash along with the \c "^attributes^" key as follows:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> h = {
    "record": {
        "^attributes^": {"type": "customer"},
        "^value^": "NO-RECORD",
    },
};
printf("%s\n", make_xml(h, XGF_ADD_FORMATTING));@endcode

    Giving the following results:

    @verbatim <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<record type="customer">NO-RECORD</record>@endverbatim

    @section xmlarrayserialization Serializing and Deserializing XML Arrays

    Arrays are serialized with repeating node names as follows:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> h = {
    "record": {
        "part": (
            "part-02-05", "part-99-23", "part-34-28",
        ),
    },
};
printf("%s\n", make_xml(h, XGF_ADD_FORMATTING));@endcode

    Producing the following results:

    @verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<record>
  <part>part-02-05</part>
  <part>part-99-23</part>
  <part>part-34-28</part>
</record>@endverbatim

    It gets a little trickier when a key should repeated at the same level in an XML string, but other keys come
    between, for example, take the following XML string:

    @verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<para>Keywords: <code>this</code>, <code>that</code>, and <code>the_other</code>.</para>@endverbatim

    It's not possible to use a list, because text is required in between. As described earlier, the \c "^value^" hash
    key can be used to serialize text in an XML string. In this case, we need to have several text nodes and several
    code nodes in a mixed-up order to give us the XML string we want. Because qore hases have unique keys (we can't
    use the same key twice in the same hash), we resort to a key naming trick that allows us to virtually duplicate
    our key names and therefore arrive at the XML string we want. We do this by appending a \c '^' character to the
    end of the key name and then some unique text. When serializing hash keys, any text after (and including) the
    \c '^' character is ignored, therefore we can add unique text to the special XML element name prefix to ensure
    that the input hash contains the data needed so that it will be serialized in the right order to the XML string
    as in the following example:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> h = {
    "para": {
        "^value^": "Keywords: ",
        "code" : "this",
        "^value^1" : ", ",
        "code^1" : "that",
        "^value^2" : ", and ",
        "code^2" : "the_other",
        "^value^3" : ".",
    },
};
printf("%s\n", make_xml(h, XGF_ADD_FORMATTING));@endcode

    Resulting in:
    @verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<para>Keywords: <code>this</code>, <code>that</code>, and <code>the_other</code>.</para>
    @endverbatim

    By ignoring the text after the second \c '^' character, unique keys can be given in the input string, and the
    above code will serialize to the XML string we want. In general, by using this convention, we can properly
    serialize multiple out-of-order keys without losing data and still have unique names for our hash keys.

    Note than when deserializing XML strings to %Qore data structures, the above rules are applied in reverse. If any
    out-of-order duplicate keys are detected, %Qore will automatically generate unique hash key names based on the
    above rules only if the @ref Qore::Xml::XPF_PRESERVE_ORDER "XPF_PRESERVE_ORDER" flag is given with the
    @ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml() "parse_xml()", @ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml_with_relaxng() "parse_xml_with_relaxng()" or
    @ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml_with_schema() "parse_xml_with_schema()" function calls.

    @section xmlcommentserialization Serializing and Deserializing XML Comments

    Comments can be serialized by using the \c "^comment^" XML element prefix (as with other special element prefixes,
    arbitrary text can appear after the \c "^comment^" prefix to make the element name unique) as in the following
    example:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> h = {
    "record": {
        "^attributes^": {"type": "customer"},
        "^comment^1": "values correspond to customer reference values",
        "^value^": "NO-RECORD",
        "^comment^2": "see docs for more info",
    },
};
printf("%s\n", make_xml(h, XGF_ADD_FORMATTING));
    @endcode

    Resulting in:
    @verbatim
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<record type="customer"><!--values correspond to customer reference values-->NO-RECORD<!--see docs for more info--></record>
    @endverbatim

    @section xmlcdataserialization Serializing and Deserializing XML CDATA

    Also note that \c CDATA text will be generated if a hash key starts with \c '^cdata'; such text will not be
    processed for ordinary escape code substitution. A carriage return splits the CDATA section so it can be
    written as a character reference between sections; this preserves the character value through XML line-ending
    normalization. Parsing retains the resulting ordered text and CDATA fragments under their corresponding keys.
    A literal \c ]]> inside supplied CDATA text remains an error.

    @section xmlelementfragments Embedding XML Element Fragments

    A hash key beginning with \c "^xml^" embeds the document element of a string containing a complete,
    well-formed XML document. Suffixes such as \c "^xml^1" and \c "^xml^2" allow multiple elements in hash
    insertion order. The document must supply its own namespace declarations; an absent default namespace
    is explicitly reset when the element is inserted under a parent. For example:

    @code{.py}
    string xml = make_xml({"shipment": {
        "^attributes^": {"xmlns": "urn:shipments"},
        "^xml^": "<quantity unit='kg'>009.00</quantity>",
    }});
    @endcode

    The shipment belongs to \c urn:shipments; quantity retains its empty namespace and decimal spelling.
    The fragment retains lexical text, attributes, ordered children, mixed text, whitespace, comments,
    processing instructions and CDATA within its document element. XML character-reference, attribute and
    line-ending normalization still applies. The XML declaration and nodes outside the document element
    are omitted. Formatting options do not add whitespace inside the fragment, and numeric-reference
    formatting does not rewrite its retained content. The fragment is converted to the requested output
    encoding; unrepresentable characters raise an encoding-conversion error.
    UTF-16 and other wider output encodings are produced by converting the complete XML result
    after markup generation; the modern and deprecated XML fragment APIs follow the same rule.

    Non-string values, NUL characters and DOCTYPE declarations raise \c MAKE-XML-ERROR. Malformed XML,
    unbound element/attribute prefixes and multiple document elements raise \c PARSE-XML-EXCEPTION.
    Parsing uses libxml2's normal resource limits and supports cooperative cancellation. Fragments are
    checked for XML well-formedness; applications must perform any required XSD validation separately.

    @since xml 2.3.0

